WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.516 --> 00:00:13.383
[MUSIC]

2
00:00:20.866 --> 00:00:26.262
In our previous video, we compared spot
and forward prices on a relative basis.

3
00:00:26.262 --> 00:00:30.958
Now in this video,
we'll explore two potential patterns,

4
00:00:30.958 --> 00:00:33.360
contango and backwardation.

5
00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.807
The usual spread between prices tells
us that forward prices are higher than

6
00:00:37.807 --> 00:00:38.858
current prices.

7
00:00:38.858 --> 00:00:43.884
This situation referred to as contango
is quite typical due to a variety

8
00:00:43.884 --> 00:00:49.678
of factors such as inflation, insurance,
storage, and investor psychology.

9
00:00:49.678 --> 00:00:53.631
However afford prices may also fall
below the current market price,

10
00:00:53.631 --> 00:00:56.776
either on a short term basis or
for a sustained period.

11
00:00:56.776 --> 00:01:01.356
When this happens, investors refer to
backwardation, which might occur when

12
00:01:01.356 --> 00:01:06.000
there's been or there's expected to be
a shortage of the product in the near term

13
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.474
yet production is why they expected to
resume in the coming weeks and months.

14
00:01:10.474 --> 00:01:15.972
Backwardation gets right to the very
heart of imminent supply and demand.

15
00:01:15.972 --> 00:01:20.774
Now at this point you may be wondering why
your next door neighbor invests in corner

16
00:01:20.774 --> 00:01:25.159
crude oil, and yet you haven't seen
their backyard crammed with stocks of

17
00:01:25.159 --> 00:01:26.983
corner flooded in petroleum.

18
00:01:26.983 --> 00:01:32.346
This is primarily because investors in the
futures market are able to transact and

19
00:01:32.346 --> 00:01:36.465
take positions in and yet
never actually own, the commodity or

20
00:01:36.465 --> 00:01:38.115
product they invest in.

21
00:01:38.115 --> 00:01:43.136
At the same time, the liquid nature of
most futures markets makes it a viable

22
00:01:43.136 --> 00:01:48.956
environment for producers to offset risk
and ultimately transact physical delivery.

23
00:01:48.956 --> 00:01:53.022
In our next video, we'll take a closer
look at futures contracts and

24
00:01:53.022 --> 00:01:56.963
provide you with an example of
a standardized crude oil contract.

25
00:01:56.963 --> 00:01:58.783
We'll look forward to you joining us then.

26
00:01:58.783 --> 00:02:02.926
[MUSIC]